---
source: crates/wows-query/src/tools.rs
assertion_line: 446
expression: specs()
---
- name: find_ships
  description: "List ships by name, tier, class or nation, including ships in closed testing (status \"test\", counted in test). Returns name, GameParams index, tier, class, nation, group and status. With a name, a ship it names as the single-ship tools read it (exact name, alias such as \"DM\", initials or former name) comes first with name_match \"named\"; ships whose names merely contain it follow with name_match \"partial\"."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      name:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name or part of it, e.g. \"moines\". A name that names one ship exactly, by an alias such as \"DM\", by its initials or by a former name lists that ship first, followed by the ships sharing its name that the other filters admit. A leading tier, as in \"T10 Fuyutsuki\", lists only ships of that tier. A qualifier such as \"old Essex\", \"[Zao]\", \"clan\", \"test\" or \"co-op\" lists that variant first. Omit to list by the other filters."
      min_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      max_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      class:
        type: string
        enum:
          - destroyer
          - cruiser
          - battleship
          - aircraft_carrier
          - submarine
        description: "A ship class. Arguments also accept the community abbreviations (DD, CA, CL, BB, CV, SS) and capitalized names."
      nation:
        type: string
        description: "Nation as GameParams spells it, e.g. \"USA\", \"Germany\", \"Japan\", \"Russia\", \"United_Kingdom\"."
      include_event_variants:
        type: boolean
        description: "Include ships players do not own: old versions, clan and event rentals, co-op bot copies, event battle ships, removed and unavailable copies. Defaults to false. They are listed after owned and test ships."
      exclude_test_ships:
        type: boolean
        description: "Leave out ships in closed testing. Defaults to false: they are listed with status \"test\". A test copy of a released ship is listed only with event variants."
      include_replicas:
        type: boolean
        description: "List premium replicas of other ships as separate ships: collaboration and recolored versions such as \"BA Montana\" of Montana, and ports to another nation such as Xuan Wu of Iowa. Defaults to false: each is named in its original's `replicas`, and listed on its own only when a nation filter leaves out the original. A name that names a replica exactly lists it either way."
- name: ship_overview
  description: "Port purchase price in credits (purchase_credits) and research XP of tech tree ships, acquisition_note when price and research fields do not tell how the ship is obtained (for ships outside the research tree: how they are obtained and their price are not in game data, so never infer a currency or say a ship is or is not sold for doubloons; tier 11 ships cost no XP), research tree predecessors and successors, group and upgrade slot count of one ship, firing range, and its armament: main and secondary battery mounts with barrels and caliber (e.g. \"2 x 2 152 mm\"), torpedo launchers and torpedoes, anti_submarine, aircraft squadrons and air strike, and consumables by slot. firing_range gives main battery and secondary range, each with builds: base (stock modules, no upgrades or captain skills), best (the best-fit build; assumed names the upgrades, captain skills and modules it takes, e.g. Gun Fire Control System Modification 2) and alternatives (e.g. \"with Spotting Aircraft active\", a conditional skill active, or the build without one upgrade). For a question about a ship's range or maximum range, give best.value and name every item of best.assumed, then the base value, and give a range consumable alternative as the range while that consumable is active. anti_submarine answers what a ship has against submarines: ship-mounted depth charge launchers (charges, depth charges per attack, damage, reload) and the depth charge air strike (aircraft, depth charges per plane, damage, charges, reload, range) are separate weapons, each stated or \"none\", plus submarine detection consumables (Hydroacoustic Search, Submarine Surveillance) with their ranges; its summary says it in one sentence. A ship without depth charge launchers may still have a depth charge air strike: read both before saying a ship cannot attack submarines. Every armament category the ship lacks is listed as \"none\". Armament is for stock modules; pass names from other_modules in modules for another hull, guns or torpedoes. other_squadrons lists squadrons of researched plane modules."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name as the user wrote it, e.g. \"Des Moines\", or a GameParams index such as \"PASC020\". A name several ships share reads as the tech tree or premium ship; a qualifier, as in \"old Essex\", \"[Zao]\", \"clan Zao\", \"test Vrijheid\" or \"Schlieffen co-op\", or a leading tier, as in \"T9 Essex\", picks another."
      modules:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "Modules whose armament to report instead of stock, e.g. [\"Hull B\"]. Omit for stock."
    required:
      - ship
- name: tech_tree_lines
  description: "Tech tree lines, each the research path from tier 1 to one tier 10 ship (tier 11 ships are not part of a line), with the research XP and purchase credits of its ships in a tier range (default 8 to 10) and their totals. path lists every ship of the route with its tier and class: take tiers from it, never from positions in the list. Ships of another class than the line's (e.g. the cruisers and destroyers before a carrier line's first carrier, which class_starts_at names) are only the research route to the line, not part of it. Lines say nothing about strength in battle: use ship_comparison for that. reset, the value of a Research Bureau reset, is given only with include_reset or a reset sort_by (reset_cost, credits_per_point, xp_per_point); it answers Research Bureau questions only. A reset rolls the line back to tier 1; each tier 5 to 10 ship of the line pays Research Points once researched again and it wins a battle or earns 300 base XP in one battle: tier 5 200, tiers 6 and 7 500, tiers 8 and 9 1,500, tier 10 6,000. A line's points depend on the tiers of the ships in its path: a line with one ship at every tier from 5 to 10 pays 10,200; most carrier lines have only tiers 6, 8 and 10 and pay 8,000, the Essex line 8,500 and submarine lines 8,700. Quote each line's research_points. Carrier lines cost less in total because they have fewer ships, so compare reset costs by cost per point, never by raw cost. reset gives research_points with points_by_ship, regrind (research XP and purchase credits of every ship from tier 2 to 10), paying_ships (the same for the ships that pay points), credits_per_point and xp_per_point (regrind over research_points), sale_credits (half the purchase price of the tier 2 to 10 ships, paid by the reset when the player owns them all) and net_credits_if_owned. Points are base values before season multipliers. sort_by orders the lines: nation (default), or cheapest first by reset_cost, credits_per_point (ties broken by XP per point), xp_per_point (ties broken by credits per point), research_xp or purchase_credits (tier range totals, each broken by the other). For the best line to reset, which weighs both the XP to research and the credits to buy, use reset_cost: each line's mean of its rank by credits_per_point and its rank by xp_per_point among all lines matching the filters (reset.reset_cost gives both ranks and mean_rank; ranking states the formula), lowest mean first, ties by credits_per_point. With reset_cost, summary states the result: lines gives one sentence per listed line in order (the first is the best line to reset), and best_in_credits and best_in_xp name the lines cheapest in each among all lines considered; answer from summary in its order and with its ranks. Remaining ties go by tier 10 ship name. branches_from names the highest-tier ship the line shares with another line; shared_with on a ship lists the tier 10 ships of the other lines through it. Costs leave out module research and purchases and upgrades. Filter by nation, by class of the tier 10 ship, or by the tier 10 ship itself, e.g. tier_10_ship \"Harugumo\" for the Harugumo line."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      nation:
        type: string
        description: "Nation as GameParams spells it, e.g. \"USA\", \"Germany\", \"Japan\", \"Russia\", \"United_Kingdom\"."
      class:
        type: string
        enum:
          - destroyer
          - cruiser
          - battleship
          - aircraft_carrier
          - submarine
        description: Class of the tier 10 ship the line ends at.
      tier_10_ship:
        type: string
        description: "The tier 10 ship the line ends at, by name or common alias, e.g. \"Harugumo\"."
      min_tier:
        type: integer
        description: Lowest tier whose ships are costed. Defaults to 8.
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 10
      max_tier:
        type: integer
        description: Highest tier whose ships are costed. Defaults to 10.
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 10
      sort_by:
        description: Order of the lines. Defaults to nation.
        oneOf:
          - type: string
            const: nation
            description: "Nation, then the tier 10 ship's name."
          - type: string
            const: credits_per_point
            description: "Cheapest first by reset purchase credits per Research Point, then reset XP per point."
          - type: string
            const: xp_per_point
            description: "Cheapest first by reset research XP per Research Point, then reset credits per point."
          - type: string
            const: reset_cost
            description: "Cheapest first by the mean of the line's ranks by credits_per_point and by xp_per_point."
          - type: string
            const: research_xp
            description: "Cheapest first by total research XP in the tier range, then total purchase credits."
          - type: string
            const: purchase_credits
            description: "Cheapest first by total purchase credits in the tier range, then total research XP."
      include_reset:
        type: boolean
        description: "Give each line its Research Bureau reset: Research Points and the cost per point. Always given when sort_by is credits_per_point or xp_per_point."
        default: false
      limit:
        type: integer
        description: Most lines to list. Defaults to 20.
        minimum: 0
- name: ship_armor
  description: "Armor of one ship by hull zone: plate thickness above the waterline (thickness_mm when every plate is equal, else thinnest_mm and thickest_mm), and each plate by its in-game name led by its zone, e.g. \"bow: Fore End Deck\", with thickness in mm. Plates of 0 mm, which only close a zone for hit detection, are left out. Thickness is nominal, not protection: what reaches the citadel depends on every plate in front of it, which armor_assessment traces."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name as the user wrote it, e.g. \"Des Moines\", or a GameParams index such as \"PASC020\". A name several ships share reads as the tech tree or premium ship; a qualifier, as in \"old Essex\", \"[Zao]\", \"clan Zao\", \"test Vrijheid\" or \"Schlieffen co-op\", or a leading tier, as in \"T9 Essex\", picks another."
    required:
      - ship
- name: ship_aircraft
  description: "Squadrons of a carrier, hybrid ship or ship with an air strike: role, aircraft, squadron size, attack flight size, payload per plane, ordnance kind (bomb, skip bomb, rocket, torpedo, depth charge), ammo type, damage per projectile, HE, SAP and AP penetration and fire chance per projectile, plus air strike planes_per_strike (the planes one strike sends, which drop the payload; squadron_size is not what one strike sends), charges, reload and range. AP bomb and rocket penetration (ordnance.ap.penetration_mm) is computed at bulletSpeed with the game's AP penetration formula; for some ordnance it differs from the port. ordnance.fire_chance_pct is the port value without skills and is 0 when the ordnance cannot start fires; fire_chance gives base and best-fit build with the skills that raise it. With thickness_mm, says whether each ordnance defeats that plate."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name, e.g. \"Midway\", \"Ise\" or \"Gouden Leeuw\"."
      thickness_mm:
        type: number
        description: "Plate thickness in mm to test each squadron's ordnance against."
    required:
      - ship
- name: ship_shells
  description: "Main and secondary battery shells of one ship: ammo type, caliber, HE/SAP penetration, the thickest whole-mm plate AP or SAP overmatches (an overmatched plate cannot ricochet it), alpha damage, muzzle velocity, fire chance, and for AP and SAP the ricochet angles from the plate normal (ricochets start, every hit ricochets) as game data states them per shell; HE does not ricochet. AP also gives fuse_time_s (time from arming to detonation) and fuse_threshold_mm (the thinnest plate that arms the fuse). ricochet_note marks a shell whose angles differ from those most shells of its ammo type share, e.g. AP whose ricochets start at 60 deg instead of 45. Each HE/SAP shell's penetration carries builds: base, best (the best-fit build, which takes Inertia Fuse for HE Shells only when it lifts the shell over its tier's armor threshold) and alternatives such as \"with Inertia Fuse for HE Shells\" with a note."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name as the user wrote it, e.g. \"Des Moines\", or a GameParams index such as \"PASC020\". A name several ships share reads as the tech tree or premium ship; a qualifier, as in \"old Essex\", \"[Zao]\", \"clan Zao\", \"test Vrijheid\" or \"Schlieffen co-op\", or a leading tier, as in \"T9 Essex\", picks another."
    required:
      - ship
- name: ap_shell_traits
  description: "Which ships have AP shells that differ from what is normal for their class, for questions such as \"which ships have improved penetration angles\" or \"which ships have short-fuse AP\". trait: improved_ricochet (\"improved penetration angles\": the shell ricochets less, starting to ricochet and always ricocheting at steeper angles than the class standard, e.g. 60 / 67.5 deg instead of 45 / 60), short_fuse (\"short fuse\" as players mean it: fuse at most half the class standard, e.g. 0.005 s against 0.033 s; detonates much sooner after penetrating, so far fewer overpenetrations on thin armor), slightly_short_fuse (below the standard but more than half of it, e.g. 0.025 s against 0.033 s), long_fuse (travels farther before detonating), low_fuse_threshold (arms on thinner plates than usual for its caliber, so fewer hits pass through thin plating without exploding) or high_fuse_threshold. Filter by class, tier and nation; battery defaults to main (secondary on request). Returns each class's standard (ricochet angles and fuse time most AP shells of the class have, from every released ship), the usual arming threshold (caliber / 6), counts of ships considered and qualifying per class with shares, and the qualifying ships, each with its shells and their values beside the standard. Every listed shell is AP by game data even when its name reads SAP-42 or P.Spr.Gr.: call it AP, never SAP. One call answers the question; no per-ship lookups are needed."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      trait:
        oneOf:
          - type: string
            const: improved_ricochet
            description: "Ricochets start at a steeper angle, always-ricochet at a steeper\nangle, or both, and neither is shallower: \"improved penetration\nangles\", the shell ricochets less."
          - type: string
            const: short_fuse
            description: "Fuse time at most half the class standard: detonates much sooner\nafter penetrating, so far fewer overpenetrations on thin armor."
          - type: string
            const: slightly_short_fuse
            description: "Fuse time below the class standard but more than half of it, such as\n0.025 s against 0.033 s."
          - type: string
            const: long_fuse
            description: "Fuse time longer than the class standard: travels farther inside the\nship before detonating, so more overpenetrations on thin armor."
          - type: string
            const: low_fuse_threshold
            description: "Arms on thinner plates than the standard for its caliber, so fewer\nhits pass through thin plating without detonating."
          - type: string
            const: high_fuse_threshold
            description: Needs thicker plates than the standard for its caliber to arm.
        description: The departure from the class standard to list.
      class:
        type: string
        enum:
          - destroyer
          - cruiser
          - battleship
          - aircraft_carrier
          - submarine
        description: "A ship class. Arguments also accept the community abbreviations (DD, CA, CL, BB, CV, SS) and capitalized names."
      min_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      max_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      nation:
        type: string
        description: "Nation as GameParams spells it, e.g. \"USA\", \"United_Kingdom\"."
      battery:
        type: string
        enum:
          - main
          - secondary
        description: Battery whose AP shells are read. Defaults to main.
      limit:
        type: integer
        description: Most ships to list. Defaults to 50.
        minimum: 0
    required:
      - trait
- name: ship_concealment
  description: "Detectability of one ship by ships, by aircraft, after firing the main battery, and after firing the main battery from smoke, each with builds: base (stock), best (best-fit modules, skills such as Concealment Expert and upgrades, listed in assumed) and alternatives (e.g. without Concealment Expert). in_smoke_evidence explains, with values per skill and upgrade, why concealment skills and upgrades do not change the in-smoke value."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name as the user wrote it, e.g. \"Des Moines\", or a GameParams index such as \"PASC020\". A name several ships share reads as the tech tree or premium ship; a qualifier, as in \"old Essex\", \"[Zao]\", \"clan Zao\", \"test Vrijheid\" or \"Schlieffen co-op\", or a leading tier, as in \"T9 Essex\", picks another."
    required:
      - ship
- name: penetration_search
  description: "Find ships whose shells, bombs or rockets defeat a plate: HE or SAP penetration at least the thickness, or AP whose caliber overmatches it (caliber > 14.3 x thickness). SAP that also overmatches the plate cannot ricochet off it and is marked \"overmatches\"; HE never overmatches. AP bombs and rockets overmatch by the same rule. Give thickness_mm, or target_ship with zone. armament selects main_battery, secondary_battery, aircraft (carrier and ship-launched squadrons) and airstrike. Without it every armament is searched, each in its own groups; groups without armament are the main battery. For carriers, planes, bombs or rockets, pass armament [\"aircraft\"] or read the aircraft groups. Without ammo, returns separate HE, SAP and AP overmatch groups per armament. Each group gives qualifying (ships that defeat the plate, listed in ships with the weapon) and considered (ships under the same filters whose armament fires that ammo), each as total and by_class, and share_pct (qualifying as a percent of considered) and share_pct_by_class, which answers \"what percent of <class>\". With a target ship and no tiers, searches the target's tier +-2."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      thickness_mm:
        type: number
        description: Plate thickness in mm to test against.
      target_ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship whose armor is the target, e.g. \"Des Moines\"."
      zone:
        type: string
        description: "Zone or plate of the target ship: \"Bow\", \"Stern\", \"Cit\", \"Cas\", \"SS\", or a plate name from ship_armor. A zone uses its thinnest plate above the waterline."
      shooter:
        type: string
        description: "Test only this ship, e.g. \"Montana\", for \"can X overmatch or pen Y\". Tier, class and nation filters then do not apply, and each group's verdict says whether the ship's best shell, bomb or rocket of that group defeats the plate."
      ammo:
        type: string
        enum:
          - AP
          - HE
          - SAP
        description: "Only this shell, bomb or rocket type. Omit to get separate HE penetration, SAP penetration and AP overmatch groups in one answer."
      ifhe:
        type: boolean
        description: Apply Inertia Fuse for HE Shells to HE shell penetration. It does not apply to bombs and rockets. Defaults to false.
      armament:
        type: array
        items:
          description: "Weapons of a ship that the search tests, each reported in its own groups."
          type: string
          enum:
            - main_battery
            - secondary_battery
            - aircraft
            - airstrike
        description: "Weapons to test, each reported in its own groups: \"main_battery\", \"secondary_battery\", \"aircraft\" (bombs and rockets of carrier squadrons and of squadrons other ships launch) and \"airstrike\" (air strike consumables). Select aircraft for questions about carriers, planes, bombs or rockets. Omit to test every armament; groups no ship under the filters fires are then left out."
      min_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "Lowest tier to include. With a target ship and no tier bounds, the target's tier +-2 is used."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      max_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "Highest tier to include. With a target ship and no tier bounds, the target's tier +-2 is used."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      class:
        type: string
        enum:
          - destroyer
          - cruiser
          - battleship
          - aircraft_carrier
          - submarine
        description: "A ship class. Arguments also accept the community abbreviations (DD, CA, CL, BB, CV, SS) and capitalized names."
      nation:
        type: string
        description: "Nation as GameParams spells it, e.g. \"USA\"."
      exclude_test_ships:
        type: boolean
        description: "Leave out ships in closed testing. Defaults to false: they are searched and counted under `test`. A test copy of a released ship is never searched."
      include_replicas:
        type: boolean
        description: "Search premium replicas of other ships (e.g. \"BA Montana\" of Montana, Xuan Wu of Iowa) as separate ships. Defaults to false: they are left out of the lists, counts and shares and named in their original's entry, unless a nation filter leaves out the original."
- name: armor_trace
  description: "Trace a shooter's shell or bomb through a target ship's 3D armor model, e.g. whether Sekiryu's AP bombs can citadel Yamato or Des Moines AP can citadel a broadside cruiser at 10 km. Casts a grid of parallel rays along the line of fire (gun range and target angle, or the bomber's dive) and runs AP through every plate with the armor viewer's simulation; HE and SAP are tested against the first plate, where SAP ricochets at steep angles unless it overmatches the plate. Without ammo, traces each shell or bomb type the weapon fires or carries. Returns one trace per type: the fraction of rays over the citadel that detonate inside it, a summary per struck zone, example rays with their plates, a verdict, and the assumptions behind it."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      shooter:
        type: string
        description: "Firing ship, e.g. \"Des Moines\" or \"Sekiryu\"."
      target_ship:
        type: string
        description: "Target ship, e.g. \"Yamato\"."
      weapon:
        description: Defaults to main_battery.
        oneOf:
          - type: string
            enum:
              - main_battery
              - secondary_battery
          - type: string
            const: bombs
            description: "AP, HE or SAP bombs of the shooter's dive bombers or air strike."
      ammo:
        type: string
        enum:
          - AP
          - HE
          - SAP
        description: Shell or bomb type. Omit to trace each type the weapon fires or carries.
      range_km:
        type: number
        description: "Gun range in km, 0 or more; 0 is point blank, flat at muzzle velocity. Required for guns, not used for bombs."
      target_angle_deg:
        type: number
        description: "Target heading away from broadside in degrees: 0 is broadside, 30 angled, 90 bow-on with the bow toward the shooter. For bombs, 0 is a dive across the ship and 90 a dive along it from bow to stern. Defaults to 0."
      target_hull:
        type: string
        description: "Target hull module, e.g. \"Hull B\". Defaults to the stock hull."
      ifhe:
        type: boolean
        description: Apply Inertia Fuse for HE Shells to gun HE penetration. Defaults to false.
      spacing_m:
        type: number
        description: "Distance between neighbouring rays in m, 0.25 to 10. Defaults to 1."
      aircraft:
        type: string
        description: "Aircraft name when the shooter carries several bomb-carrying squadrons, e.g. \"Ryusei\". Without it, each bomb type comes from stock dive bombers before researched or jet dive bombers, and those before the air strike."
    required:
      - shooter
      - target_ship
- name: citadel_profile
  description: "Shape and exposure of one ship's citadel from its 3D armor model: share of the citadel face above the waterline per side, roof height, slope of the citadel plates by height band (vertical, inclined belt, sloped deck, flat deck), whether it is a turtleback, and its height along the ship. For broadside flat fire, and for a shooter's main battery AP at range_km when given, the armor in front of the citadel (effective thickness spread and the least, median and most armored plate stacks with overmatch, ricochet and fuse events, over rays without a glancing strike, with the glancing rays counted apart) and how often the citadel plate is struck past 60 deg or past the ricochet angles."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship whose citadel is described, e.g. \"Yamato\"."
      hull:
        type: string
        description: "Hull module, e.g. \"Hull B\". Defaults to the stock hull."
      shooter:
        type: string
        description: "Ship whose main battery AP is fired at the citadel, e.g. \"Des Moines\". Needs range_km."
      range_km:
        type: number
        description: "Range in km for the shooter's plunging fire."
      spacing_m:
        type: number
        description: "Distance between neighbouring rays in m, 0.25 to 10. Defaults to 0.5."
    required:
      - ship
- name: armor_assessment
  description: "How well one ship's armor protects it, for questions such as \"is X's armor good\" or \"is X tanky\". Traces the ship's 3D armor model against shooter groups chosen from its matchmaking tiers (the most common destroyer, light cruiser, heavy cruiser and battleship AP calibers, cruiser HE and SAP) at broadside, 30 and 45 deg angled, in close (5-10 km), moderate (12-15 km) and long (15 km and beyond) range bands; shooters that cannot reach a band are listed as excluded. Per band, group and aspect: citadel, penetration, overpenetration, ricochet, shatter and fuse-armed rates with ray counts, ranked among released ships of its class within one tier. Properties: citadel roof and floor against the waterline, share of the citadel side above water, turtleback, the citadel face above water, and plates that arm the fuse of AP that would otherwise overpenetrate. Ends with a verdict built from those numbers. Destroyers and submarines have no citadel: citadel_metrics says citadel rates do not apply to them, and a missing citadel is no weakness."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship whose armor is assessed, e.g. \"Khabarovsk\"."
      hull:
        type: string
        description: "Hull module, e.g. \"Hull B\". Defaults to the stock hull. Peers are compared on their stock hulls."
    required:
      - ship
- name: ship_comparison
  description: "Compare the ships of one class and tier (or tier range) on the factors that decide their strength, for questions such as \"worst tier 10 battleship\", \"is Des Moines good\", \"why is X considered bad\" or \"is Incomparable better than C. Colombo\". Peers are always every ship of the class and tiers, of every nation. Name the ships asked about in ships: each named ship gets a full entry, its category (tech_tree, or premium for premium and special ships, which belong to no tech tree line), per factor the value on its best-fit build, its rank in the group and the share of the group it beats, and outliers: values better or worse than 90% of the group, plus signals, consumables and special abilities. With two or more ships of one class named (\"is A better than B\"), head_to_head compares each pair factor by factor, every factor counting the same: the factors each ship is better on and those tied, each ship's largest leads, and a verdict: \"On paper A is better\" when A leads on at least twice as many factors as B, \"Nearly the same\" when more than half are tied, else \"Mixed\"; quote the verdict and its counts. Few ships have a unique upgrade: a value, leader or outlier takes one only where it says \"with unique upgrade <name>\"; never say a ship's value takes a unique upgrade unless it is marked so, and unique_upgrade appears only for a ship that has one. Without ships, only the group summary is returned: ship_names (every ranked ship) and leaders. leaders gives per factor the best and worst ships of the group, three each without ships named and one each with them, each with its nation and value and with \"premium\" or \"test\" for a ship not of the tech tree, e.g. \"Colbert (France, light cruiser, premium)\": a premium ship heads no line to research. torpedo_leaders the same per torpedo option, left out when no named ship carries torpedoes. Cruisers are typed light (below 170 mm main battery caliber), heavy (170 to 260 mm) or large (above): named cruisers carry cruiser_type, leader labels name the type, and each cruiser value also ranks among the group's cruisers of its type, e.g. \"4.84 (rank 14/56, p73; heavy cruisers rank 1/23, p91)\". Judge a cruiser against its own type first. Factors: health, Repair Party heal; main battery reload, range, damage per minute, salvo damage and shell flight time per ammo, turret traverse, dispersion and sigma, fire starts, barrels bearing ahead and astern and the angles at which all bear; secondary damage and range; torpedo damage per minute, torpedoes per minute with loader limits, reload, launch angle off the bow and tubes per broadside; anti-aircraft damage and range; concealment by ships and aircraft; speed, rudder shift, turning circle. Per torpedo option: speed, range, damage, damage at the end of its run and damage by distance, flooding coefficient, detectability, reaction time and damage per minute, ranked among every torpedo option of the group. Cruisers and battleships also get battleship AP citadel and overpenetration rates, the share of matchmaking battleships that overmatch the bow and stern, and, for ships named, matchups against every shooter group of armor_assessment (not nominal plate thickness); destroyers get no armor judgment. A battleship share above half is a weakness whatever its rank, and its value leads with \"weakness:\": report it as a weakness first and its rank second, never as \"only\" a share. signals give the play style the numbers point to, such as kiting or bow-in play. Released and test ships are ranked; replicas are named in their original's entry. No single score is given: weigh the factors for the question."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      class:
        type: string
        enum:
          - destroyer
          - cruiser
          - battleship
          - aircraft_carrier
          - submarine
        description: "Class compared. Optional with ships: then the class of the first ship named."
      tier:
        type: integer
        description: "One tier, e.g. 10. Use min_tier and max_tier for a range, e.g. 9 to 11 for a matchmaking band or 1 to 11 for every tier of the class."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      min_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      max_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      ships:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "Ships to give full entries, e.g. [\"Incomparable\", \"C. Colombo\"]; name the ship asked about. Without ships only a summary of the group is returned. Ranks still compare each with the whole group, which also takes in every ship named. Without tiers, the group spans the tiers of the ships named. Matchups against shooter groups are listed only for ships named."
      reference_range_km:
        type: number
        description: "Range in km at which dispersion and shell flight time are compared. Defaults to 15 for battleships, 12 for cruisers and 10 for other classes."
- name: turtleback_search
  description: "Which ships have a turtleback: an armored deck sloping down behind a thicker belt to meet it near or below the waterline, read from each ship's 3D armor model. Filter by tier, class and nation. Returns considered and qualifying counts per class and per nation with shares, and the qualifying ships with deck thickness, slope and belt thickness. Ships sharing one armor model, such as collaboration copies, count once."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      min_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      max_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      class:
        type: string
        enum:
          - destroyer
          - cruiser
          - battleship
          - aircraft_carrier
          - submarine
        description: "A ship class. Arguments also accept the community abbreviations (DD, CA, CL, BB, CV, SS) and capitalized names."
      nation:
        type: string
        description: "Nation as GameParams spells it, e.g. \"Germany\", \"USA\", \"Russia\"."
      exclude_test_ships:
        type: boolean
        description: Leave out ships in closed testing. Defaults to false.
- name: ship_stats
  description: "Port stat card of one ship with stock modules, followed by stats computed from it: main battery barrels, salvo damage and damage per minute per ammo type, dispersion at the stock firing range, torpedo tubes, time to use every charge of a consumable, fire starts per minute per armament and in total, and fire and flooding. Fire chance reduction is how much less likely any shell is to set the ship on fire than its listed fire chance; it comes from the hull and grows with tier (49.95% on a tier 10 hull and on every aircraft carrier, 13.32% on a stock tier 5 hull and 16.65% on its upgraded hull) and Fire Prevention Expert multiplies what is left by 0.9. Also fires that can burn at once (usually 4, 1 on submarines; Fire Prevention Expert merges two into one), fire and flooding duration, damage per second in percent of maximum health and damage of one whole fire or flood. Without skills and upgrades, build_stats gives main battery reload and range, secondary range, torpedo reload, torpedoes_per_minute, the damage per minute and per second of each main battery ammo, secondary gun group and torpedo, and fires_per_minute, each with builds: base, best-fit build (assumed) and alternatives; main battery range lists a range consumable such as Spotting Aircraft as the alternative \"with Spotting Aircraft active\". build_stats.speed gives top speed on the surface and, for submarines, at periscope depth and submerged (the fastest of operating and maximum depth), with alternatives for conditional skills (e.g. Enlarged Propeller Shaft: +18% on the surface and at periscope depth only, while less than half the dive capacity remains) and for the Sierra Mike signal (+5% at every depth), alone and combined; Engine Boost is not included. torpedoes_per_minute counts the tubes, and the loader groups when the game reloads only some launchers at a time (submarine groups name bow, stern or rotating), and states the limit. Torpedo rates are against one target at the bearing where the most tubes bear, counting only the launchers that turn to it, so a ship with launchers on both sides brings one side to bear; all_launchers_ceiling is every launcher firing at once (submarine rates always count every launcher). A ship with the Torpedo Reload Booster consumable also gets torpedo_reload_booster: the reload it sets, charges, action time, cooldown (with the ship's special ability active as an alternative when it changes them), and torpedoes_per_minute and torpedo_damage averaged over one booster cycle with the booster used whenever ready. A ship with a consumable whose buff shortens the launcher reload while active (Auxiliary and Torpedo Weaponry Coordination) gets torpedo_reload_buffs: the reload factor while active, charges, action time, cooldown, and torpedoes_per_minute and torpedo_damage averaged over one cycle with it used whenever ready. torpedo reload is the launchers' own reload, a different thing from these consumables. A ship with a Main Battery Reload Booster gets the alternative \"with Main Battery Reload Booster active\" in each main battery ammo's damage rate builds: the rate while the booster runs, the reload time times its main_battery_reload_factor (ship_consumables gives its charges or time-based capacity); with special_ability_active it is the rate with both the special ability (e.g. Combat Instructions) and the booster active. Damage rates are theoretical maxima: every shell or torpedo hits for full damage, without fires, flooding, citadel or armor. Gun damage rates and fire starts are against one target at the bearing where they peak (bearing, degrees clockwise from the bow: 90 is the starboard beam, 270 the port beam), counting only the mounts whose firing sectors cover it, with by_bearing at bow-on, 30, 45, 60, 90, 120, 135, 150 and stern-on for both sides; all_barrels_ceiling, all_mounts_ceiling and all_launchers_ceiling are every mount or launcher firing at once, which no single target sees, so never quote them as what a ship does to one target. fires_per_minute counts every armament that can start fires: main battery ammo with a fire chance, each secondary gun group (and secondaries, every group together), carrier and hybrid squadrons and air strikes, with projectiles per minute, the fire chance of one projectile and the chance one salvo starts a fire; its total is what answers which ship starts the most fires, and Inertia Fuse for HE Shells appears as an alternative because it halves shell fire chance. With captain skills and upgrades, lists the stats they change, from and to, and which skill or upgrade caused each change. special_ability_active switches on the ship's F key ability (ship_special_ability says what it is): Combat Instructions apply their effects, e.g. a longer secondary range; Burst Fire or Modified Shells switch the main battery, and build_stats then gives burst_fire (shots_per_burst, shot_interval, reload_after_burst, cycle) and each main battery ammo's damage_per_minute over a full burst cycle with during_burst, the rate while a burst fires; changes lists what the ability changes against the standard stock card. With commander (a unique commander such as \"Jean-Jacques Honore\"), the commander's improved skills replace the standard ones in skills (marked improved) and build_stats, and talents lists each talent that changes a stat: its activation condition, the activations counted and the changes. Talents are never applied to changes or stats."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name, e.g. \"Des Moines\"."
      skills:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "Captain skills by name or acronym, e.g. [\"IFHE\", \"Concealment Expert\"]. Resolved in the ship's class tree."
      upgrades:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "Upgrades by name, e.g. [\"Main Battery Modification 2\"]. One per slot."
      conditions_met:
        type: boolean
        description: "Treat conditional skill triggers as met, e.g. an enemy in secondary range for Close Quarters Combat. Defaults to false."
      hp_percent:
        type: number
        description: Remaining health in percent for health-scaled effects such as Adrenaline Rush. Defaults to full health.
      special_ability_active:
        type: boolean
        description: "Switch on the ship's special ability (its F key): Combat Instructions apply their effects; an alternative fire mode such as Burst Fire switches the main battery to it, with its reload, shells and effects. Defaults to false."
      commander:
        type: string
        description: "Unique commander, e.g. \"Jean-Jacques Honore\": their improved versions replace the standard skills taken, and their talents that change a stat are listed under `talents`."
      full_card:
        type: boolean
        description: "Include the full stat card. Defaults to true without skills or upgrades and false with them, when only the changes are listed."
    required:
      - ship
- name: ship_special_ability
  description: "What one ship's special ability does, the ability players call \"the F key\": Combat Instructions (in-game name; a meter filled by events such as main battery hits or potential damage received, then activated for a set duration with effects such as faster secondary reload, or started automatically on some ships) and the alternative main battery fire mode (\"Burst Fire\", \"Modified Shells\" or \"Burst Fire and Modified Shells\": shots per burst, interval between shots, reload after the burst, cycle time, shells and effects). Gives charge conditions with progress per event, progress loss, duration, effects, and uses per battle (no charges or cooldown: each use needs the meter refilled). Ships without either say so. Values are for no captain skills or upgrades; ship_stats with special_ability_active gives stats with the ability on."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name as the user wrote it, e.g. \"Des Moines\", or a GameParams index such as \"PASC020\". A name several ships share reads as the tech tree or premium ship; a qualifier, as in \"old Essex\", \"[Zao]\", \"clan Zao\", \"test Vrijheid\" or \"Schlieffen co-op\", or a leading tier, as in \"T9 Essex\", picks another."
    required:
      - ship
- name: special_ability_search
  description: "Which ships have a special ability (the F key): Combat Instructions, an alternative main battery fire mode, or Burst Fire, optionally filtered by words of the ability name or an effect (e.g. \"secondary battery firing range\", \"reload\") and by tier, class and nation. Lists every qualifying ship, released and test apart, with the ability, its duration or burst timing and effects, and counts per class and kind with shares of the ships considered."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      kind:
        oneOf:
          - type: string
            enum:
              - combat_instructions
          - type: string
            const: alternative_fire_mode
            description: "Every alternative main battery fire mode: Burst Fire, Modified Shells, or both."
          - type: string
            const: burst_fire
            description: Alternative fire modes that fire bursts.
        description: A kind of ship ability.
      effect:
        type: string
        description: "Words of the ability name or of one of its effects, e.g. \"secondary battery firing range\", \"reload\", \"Modified Secondary Munitions\"."
      min_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      max_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      class:
        type: string
        enum:
          - destroyer
          - cruiser
          - battleship
          - aircraft_carrier
          - submarine
        description: "A ship class. Arguments also accept the community abbreviations (DD, CA, CL, BB, CV, SS) and capitalized names."
      nation:
        type: string
        description: "Nation as GameParams spells it, e.g. \"USA\", \"Spain\", \"Pan_America\"."
      exclude_test_ships:
        type: boolean
        description: "Leave out ships in closed testing. Defaults to false: they are listed under `test`."
      include_event_variants:
        type: boolean
        description: "Include event, clan-battle and retired variants. Defaults to false."
- name: ship_firing_arcs
  description: "Which guns and torpedo tubes of one ship bear on a target at each bearing: barrels bearing bow-on, stern-on (within 30 degrees) and on each broadside, where all barrels bear or never, damage of one salvo per ammo type, fires per minute and the fire chance of one salvo assuming every shell hits, and main battery dispersion at a range. dispersion.range_km is the distance of that ellipse, by default the maximum range of the build given (stock without skills and upgrades); it is not the ship's best firing range, which ship_overview firing_range gives with its builds. salvo_fire_chance_pct is the chance a salvo starts at least one fire, 1 - (1 - p)^n over its n bearing shells with p the chance per hit; use it to answer \"fire chance of a salvo\" rather than adding shell chances. Optional skills, upgrades and modules; guns or torpedoes that fit only another hull need that hull in modules too. Optional target_ship applies the target's fire resistance: each shell's fire chance on that target is its fire chance times the target's fire_chance_factor, from its hull's tier and from target_skills and target_upgrades such as Fire Prevention Expert."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name, e.g. \"Des Moines\"."
      modules:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "Modules to mount instead of stock, e.g. [\"Hull B\"]. Omit for stock."
      skills:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "Captain skills by name or acronym, e.g. [\"Demolition Expert\"]. They change reload and fire chance."
      upgrades:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "Upgrades by name, e.g. [\"Main Battery Modification 3\"]. One per slot."
      target_ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship being fired at, e.g. \"Colbert\". Its hull's fire resistance and its build scale the fire chance. Omit to use the shell's fire chance alone."
      target_modules:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "The target's modules, e.g. [\"Hull B\"]. Omit for stock; an upgraded hull usually resists fire more."
      target_skills:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "The target's captain skills, e.g. [\"Fire Prevention Expert\"]."
      target_upgrades:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "The target's upgrades, e.g. [\"Damage Control System Modification 1\"]."
      range_km:
        type: number
        description: "Firing range in km for the dispersion ellipse, 0 or more; 0 gives the ellipse at the muzzle and ranges past the main battery's maximum use the maximum. Defaults to the maximum range."
    required:
      - ship
- name: dispersion_at_range
  description: "Main battery or secondary battery shell dispersion of one ship at a firing distance, horizontal and vertical in meters, on the base build and with each fire control module, upgrade, captain skill and consumable that lengthens the battery's range (e.g. Spotting Aircraft, range upgrades and skills) or tightens its accuracy (e.g. Aiming Systems Modification 1), plus the longest range build and that build with accuracy upgrades. Use it for questions such as \"does more range improve dispersion\", \"Yamato dispersion at 15 km with spotter\" or \"secondary accuracy of Hannover at 8 km\". Horizontal dispersion depends only on the distance and accuracy modifiers; vertical dispersion is horizontal times a ratio set by the distance as a fraction of max range, so a longer max range shrinks vertical dispersion at the same distance and leaves horizontal unchanged. A row with condition holds only then, e.g. Manual Secondary Battery Aiming at the priority target after sustained fire. conclusion states this for the ship in one or two sentences; builds beyond_max_range cannot fire that far."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name, e.g. \"Yamato\"."
      range_km:
        type: number
        description: "Firing distance in km, more than 0, e.g. 12."
      battery:
        type: string
        enum:
          - main_battery
          - secondary
        description: "\"main_battery\" (default) or \"secondary\"."
      modules:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "Modules to mount instead of stock, e.g. [\"Hull B\"]. Omit for stock."
    required:
      - ship
      - range_km
- name: stat_names
  description: "Every stat stat_search can compare, as \"name (unit, per item): label\". Includes armor zone plate thickness, which is nominal and not protection (use armor_assessment or ship_comparison for that), stats computed from the card, such as total main battery barrels, salvo damage, damage per minute, dispersion at the stock firing range and sigma, and main battery AP and SAP ricochet angles, which game data states per shell."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
- name: stat_search
  description: "Find ships whose stats meet every condition, e.g. [{\"stat\": \"artillery.reload_time\", \"op\": \"<\", \"value\": 5}], or main battery AP whose ricochets start later than most, [{\"stat\": \"artillery.ap.ricochet_start\", \"op\": \">\", \"value\": 45}], or rank ships by one stat. Get stat names from stat_names. With conditions: lists ships that qualify stock, and ships that need modules, upgrades or captain skills, with the fewest needed, and counts of ships considered and qualifying by class, with share_pct and share_pct_by_class, which answers \"what percent of <class>\". For \"best\", \"lowest\", \"highest\", \"worst\" or \"most\" questions, give rank instead of guessing thresholds, e.g. the best concealment tier 10 destroyer is {\"rank\": {\"stat\": \"visibility.sea_detection\", \"order\": \"asc\"}, \"class\": \"destroyer\", \"min_tier\": 10, \"max_tier\": 10} and the worst concealment is the same with \"order\": \"desc\": one call lists the top ships in order, each with its value on the ranked build, its base value (stock hull and modules, no captain skills, no upgrades), the build, ties, and for stats with several rows the row the value comes from, e.g. \"main battery 457 mm HE\". Ship labels end \", premium\" for premium and special ships, which belong to no tech tree line, and \", test\" for ships in testing. Without rank.build every ship is ranked on the build players run for the stat, the one that makes it better (lower detection, reload and dispersion; higher damage, range and health), in either order. Give \"build\": \"base\" for base values such as base detection, and \"lowest\" or \"highest\" only for how far skills and upgrades can push a stat either way; never rank one build and quote another column. Low tiers lead many stats across all tiers, so give min_tier and max_tier when the question names or implies a tier, and say which tiers you ranked when it does not. Conditions and rank combine: only qualifying ships are ranked. Consumable stats (consumable.charges, consumable.work_time, consumable.reload_time, ...) have one row per consumable, named with its English name and type; the type is the qualifier, e.g. \"artilleryBoosters\" for the Main Battery Reload Booster. Time-based consumables, switched on and off and drawing on a pool of seconds of use instead of charges (e.g. 20 de Julio's Main Battery Reload Booster, Sibir's Repair Party), are the only ones with consumable.capacity: [{\"stat\": \"consumable.capacity\", \"op\": \">=\", \"value\": 0}] lists every ship with one, each with the consumable and its capacity in seconds. For how fast submarines are underwater, rank underwater_mobility.speed, the submerged speed at maximum depth; mobility.speed is the surface speed, and underwater_mobility.periscope_speed (just below the surface) equals the surface speed on almost every submarine, so neither is the underwater speed. Anti-submarine weapons (ASW) rank in one call over every class: asw.depth_charge.* (ship-mounted depth charges: damage, splash_radius, per_attack, damage_per_attack, charges, reload), asw.airstrike.* (the depth charge air strike: damage, splash_radius, per_strike, damage_per_strike, charges, reload, range) and asw.best_damage_per_attack (the stronger of the two weapons per attack, the row naming it); for \"worst ASW\" rank asw.best_damage_per_attack asc. Most surface ships have one or both weapons; submarines have neither and are not ranked. Splash radius is in the game world's meters, the scale of ship models. These are base values without skills or upgrades. For which ships start the most fires, rank fires_per_minute.total: fire starts against one target at the bearing where the ship starts the most, counting only the gun mounts that bear there, plus squadrons and air strikes; each ranked ship carries fires_by_armament (bearing, total, main_battery, secondary, aircraft), so no further call is needed for the split. fires_per_minute.secondary ranks every secondary group together against one target and fires_per_minute.secondary_group one group; fires_per_minute.total_all_mounts and fires_per_minute.secondary_all_mounts count every mount at once, a ceiling no single target sees. artillery.shells.burn_chance is the chance of one main battery shell only."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      conditions:
        type: array
        items:
          description: One condition on a stat.
          type: object
          properties:
            stat:
              description: "Stat name from stat_names, e.g. \"artillery.reload_time\" or \"armor.citadel.max\"."
              type: string
            op:
              description: "How a stat value compares to a condition's threshold."
              type: string
              enum:
                - "<"
                - "<="
                - ">"
                - ">="
                - "="
                - "!="
            value:
              description: Threshold in the unit stat_names lists for the stat.
              type: number
            qualifier:
              description: "Only the row with this qualifier: ammo type for shells (\"HE\", \"AP\", \"SAP\"), torpedo name as values show it, secondary mount or consumable name. Omit to accept any row."
              type: string
          additionalProperties: false
          required:
            - stat
            - op
            - value
        description: Conditions that must all hold. Optional when rank is given.
      rank:
        type: object
        additionalProperties: false
        properties:
          stat:
            type: string
            description: "Stat name from stat_names, e.g. \"visibility.sea_detection\"."
          order:
            type: string
            enum:
              - asc
              - desc
            description: "\"asc\": lowest first. \"desc\": highest first."
          qualifier:
            type: string
            description: "Only the row with this qualifier, as for conditions. Omit to rank each ship by its lowest or highest row, as build picks."
          limit:
            type: integer
            description: Ships listed. Defaults to 10.
            minimum: 1
            maximum: 25
          build:
            description: "The build each ship is ranked on. Omit it to rank each ship on the build players run for the stat, the one that makes it better whatever the order: lower detection, reload, dispersion and flight time; higher damage, range and health. So \"desc\" on visibility.sea_detection lists the worst concealment as players build their ships. \"base\": stock hull and modules, no captain skills, no upgrades, for base values such as base detection. \"lowest\" or \"highest\": the build that lowers or raises the stat most, e.g. how bad a stat can get. A stat with no better direction ranks on the build that moves it furthest in the order."
            oneOf:
              - type: string
                const: base
                description: "Stock hull and modules, or the first modules that have the stat\nwhen stock lacks it; no captain skills or upgrades."
              - type: string
                const: lowest
                description: "The modules, captain skills and upgrades that lower the stat most."
              - type: string
                const: highest
                description: "The modules, captain skills and upgrades that raise the stat most."
        required:
          - stat
          - order
        description: "Order ships by one stat on each ship's best-fit build, for \"best\", \"lowest\", \"highest\" or \"most\" questions. With conditions, ranks only the ships that meet them."
      min_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      max_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      class:
        type: string
        enum:
          - destroyer
          - cruiser
          - battleship
          - aircraft_carrier
          - submarine
        description: "A ship class. Arguments also accept the community abbreviations (DD, CA, CL, BB, CV, SS) and capitalized names."
      nation:
        type: string
        description: "Nation as GameParams spells it, e.g. \"USA\"."
      include_event_variants:
        type: boolean
        description: "Include ships players do not own: old versions, clan and event rentals, co-op bot copies, event battle ships, removed and unavailable copies. Defaults to false."
      exclude_test_ships:
        type: boolean
        description: "Leave out ships in closed testing. Defaults to false: they are searched and counted under `test`. A test copy of a released ship is searched only with event variants."
      include_replicas:
        type: boolean
        description: "Search premium replicas of other ships (e.g. \"BA Montana\" of Montana, Xuan Wu of Iowa) as separate ships. Defaults to false: they are left out of the results and counts and named in their original's entry, unless a nation filter leaves out the original."
- name: captain_skill
  description: "One captain skill by name, abbreviation (IFHE, SI, CE, SE, PT, IFA, AR, DE, BoS, RPF) or former name (BFT, AFT): description, point cost and effects per ship class, or for one ship's class with ship."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      skill:
        type: string
        description: "Skill name, e.g. \"Inertia Fuse for HE Shells\", a common abbreviation such as IFHE, SI, CE, SE, PT, IFA, AR, DE, BoS, or a former name such as BFT."
      class:
        type: string
        enum:
          - destroyer
          - cruiser
          - battleship
          - aircraft_carrier
          - submarine
        description: Only describe the skill for this class.
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Only describe the skill for this ship's class, e.g. \"GK\". Overrides class."
    required:
      - skill
- name: skill_tree
  description: "The captain skill tree of one ship class, skill names by point cost."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      class:
        type: string
        enum:
          - destroyer
          - cruiser
          - battleship
          - aircraft_carrier
          - submarine
        description: "A ship class. Arguments also accept the community abbreviations (DD, CA, CL, BB, CV, SS) and capitalized names."
    required:
      - class
- name: upgrade
  description: "One upgrade (modernization) by name: slot, kind (regular, consumable_modification, or unique for a unique upgrade made for specific ships), credit price, effects per class and which tiers, nations and ships it fits."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      upgrade:
        type: string
        description: "Upgrade name, e.g. \"Main Battery Modification 2\" or \"Concealment System Modification 1\"."
      ship:
        type: string
        description: Ship to check the upgrade against; also selects the class for effects.
    required:
      - upgrade
- name: ship_upgrades
  description: "Every upgrade that fits one ship, by slot, with kind, price and effects. A ship with a unique upgrade lists it in its slot with kind \"unique\" and, under unique_upgrades, which ships it fits and the regular upgrades of the slot it replaces."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name as the user wrote it, e.g. \"Des Moines\", or a GameParams index such as \"PASC020\". A name several ships share reads as the tech tree or premium ship; a qualifier, as in \"old Essex\", \"[Zao]\", \"clan Zao\", \"test Vrijheid\" or \"Schlieffen co-op\", or a leading tier, as in \"T9 Essex\", picks another."
    required:
      - ship
- name: unique_upgrades
  description: "Unique (legendary) upgrades, each made for specific ships, for questions such as \"which ships have a unique upgrade\" or \"what does Colombo's unique upgrade do\": name, slot, the ships it fits, effects, and the regular upgrades of the slot it replaces. Filter by tier, class and nation of the ships. Only the ships listed here have one. Builds in the other tools name a unique upgrade, marked \"(unique upgrade)\", only where the build takes it, and then also give the build without it."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      min_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      max_tier:
        type: integer
        description: "A ship tier, 1 to 11. Arguments also accept \"supership\" for tier 11."
        minimum: 1
        maximum: 11
      class:
        type: string
        enum:
          - destroyer
          - cruiser
          - battleship
          - aircraft_carrier
          - submarine
        description: "A ship class. Arguments also accept the community abbreviations (DD, CA, CL, BB, CV, SS) and capitalized names."
      nation:
        type: string
        description: "Nation as GameParams spells it, e.g. \"USA\"."
- name: ship_consumables
  description: "Consumables of one ship by slot with charges, reload, action time, detection ranges and heal rate. Each slot carries one consumable in battle: a slot with choice (\"choose one of: Surveillance Radar, Fighters, Spotting Aircraft\") carries only the option the player picks, never several at once. A time-based consumable (e.g. 20 de Julio's Main Battery Reload Booster) has no charges or action time: capacity_s is its seconds of use per battle, spent in activations of at least min_activation_s with reload_s between them. A Main Battery Reload Booster gives main_battery_reload_factor, the main battery reload time multiplier while active (0.5 halves it); ship_stats gives the damage rates with it active. A Repair Party gives heal: HP and percent of max_hp restored by one charge (per_charge = heal_percent_per_s x action_time_s x max_hp) and by every charge (per_battle); quote these for how much a Repair Party heals, never the rate times the charges. They are ceilings: only repairable damage is restored. A consumable that is used on an allied ship (e.g. Engineering Crew Assistance) or applies a buff carries buff: who it can be used on, range, and its effects on the ally and on your own ship. Without skills and upgrades, each consumable carries builds for action time, charges, capacity, reload, smoke lifetime and Repair Party heal_per_charge and heal_per_battle: base, best (consumable upgrades and skills such as Superintendent, Consumable Enhancements and Survivability Expert, listed in assumed) and alternatives. consumable narrows to one, e.g. \"radar\", \"hydro\", \"DCP\"; choice still names the slot's other options. With skills and upgrades, applies them instead."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship name, e.g. \"Des Moines\"."
      consumable:
        type: string
        description: "Only this consumable, e.g. \"radar\", \"hydro\", \"Repair Party\", \"DCP\"."
      skills:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "Captain skills by name or abbreviation, e.g. [\"SI\"], applied to the consumable stats. Without skills and upgrades, each consumable lists its base and best-fit build values."
      upgrades:
        type: array
        items:
          type: string
        description: "Upgrades by name, e.g. [\"Engine Boost Modification 1\"], applied to the consumable stats."
    required:
      - ship
- name: healing_sources
  description: "Every way one ship can restore HP, for questions such as \"how can Akizuki heal without a Repair Party\": its own consumables that heal it (Repair Party, and heals its squadrons drop on it), consumables that allied ships and carrier squadrons use on it (e.g. Engineering Crew Assistance, Repair Assistance) with the ships that carry them and their values, talents of unique commanders who can command it with what activates them, and game mode buffs such as Arms Race HP regeneration. Without ship, lists the consumables that heal allied ships and the ships that carry them."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship to list every way it can restore HP, e.g. \"Akizuki\". Omit to list the consumables that heal allied ships and the ships that carry them."
- name: ship_hit_locations
  description: "Damage saturation of one ship, for questions such as \"how does saturation work\", \"what is French saturation\", \"does X have French saturation\" or \"how much HP is in X's superstructure\". Lists per hull each hull section (bow, stern, casemate or midsection, superstructure, auxiliary rooms, citadel, and the hull pool most of them also drain) with its pool HP and share of ship HP, whether it saturates, the share of an AP shell's listed damage a penetration deals there (penetration_damage_pct) and once the section is saturated (saturated_damage_pct, half as much), and the share of damage Repair Party can restore there, with a plain explanation of saturation. French saturation is a destroyer trait: the midsection of most French destroyers and a few others saturates, while most destroyers' midsections never do; French battleships and cruisers saturate like every other nation's."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Ship to list the hull sections of, e.g. \"Republique\"."
    required:
      - ship
- name: commander
  description: "One unique (legendary) commander by name, e.g. \"Yamamoto Isoroku\", \"Halsey\", \"Jean-Jacques Honore\", \"John Doe\": the nations and ships they serve on, their improved captain skills with the standard and improved effects per class, and their talents: battle types, what activates them, effects per activation, activations per battle and the effects after every activation. With ship, only that ship's class. Pass the commander to ship_stats to apply the improved skills to a ship."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      commander:
        type: string
        description: "Unique commander name, e.g. \"Yamamoto Isoroku\", \"Halsey\", \"Jean-Jacques Honore\" or \"John Doe\"."
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Only describe the commander for this ship's class, e.g. \"Yamato\"."
    required:
      - commander
- name: commander_search
  description: "Find unique commanders by what their improved skills or talents do, e.g. effect \"restores HP\", \"reload\", \"fires\", by nation, or by a ship they can command. Each commander lists only the improved skills and talents that match. Omit effect to list every unique commander with improved skills or talents."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      effect:
        type: string
        description: "Words an improved skill or talent must mention in its name, condition or effects, e.g. \"restores HP\", \"heal\", \"reload\", \"speed\", \"fires\". Omit to list every unique commander."
      nation:
        type: string
        description: "Nation as GameParams spells it, e.g. \"Japan\", \"USA\", \"United_Kingdom\", \"Pan_Asia\"."
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Only commanders who serve on this ship, with effects for its class."
- name: game_mode_buffs
  description: "Buffs a game mode hands out in battle, e.g. Arms Race: each buff with its effects per number of stacks, per ship class or for one ship's class. Picking up a buff applies it to every ship of the team; picking up the same buff again adds a stack, up to max_stacks."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      mode:
        type: string
        description: "Game mode, e.g. \"Arms Race\". Omit for every mode with buffs."
      ship:
        type: string
        description: "Only the effects for this ship's class, e.g. \"Akizuki\"."
- name: gameparams_query
  description: "Run a jq filter (jaq dialect) over raw GameParams entries, for values the other tools do not report. select is one entry {\"param\": \"PASC020\"} (name, index or id), one ship {\"ship\": \"Des Moines\"}, or a set {\"set\": {\"type\": \"Ship\", \"nation\": \"USA\", \"species\": \"Cruiser\", \"tier\": 10}} of at most limit entries. The filter runs once per entry with the entry object as input; entries without output are omitted. Entries are large: start with `keys` (no leading dot; `.keys` reads a field named keys), then `.Field | keys`, then pick fields. Output is cut to max_bytes; when truncated, hint says how to narrow the filter. The filter has 5 seconds; env is not available. A ship's costGold is a nominal value, not what the ship sells for; never quote it as a price."
  input_schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    properties:
      select:
        oneOf:
          - type: object
            properties:
              param:
                type: string
            required:
              - param
            additionalProperties: false
            description: "One entry by param name (PASC020_Des_Moines_1948), index (PASC020)\nor numeric id."
          - type: object
            properties:
              ship:
                type: string
            required:
              - ship
            additionalProperties: false
            description: "One ship by English name, as the ship tools resolve it."
          - type: object
            properties:
              set:
                description: Entries matching every given field. Text matches without regard to case.
                type: object
                properties:
                  type:
                    description: "`typeinfo.type`, e.g. Ship, Projectile, Gun, Modernization, Ability,\nCrew, Unit, Exterior."
                    type: string
                  nation:
                    description: "`typeinfo.nation`, e.g. USA, Japan, Germany, Russia."
                    type: string
                  species:
                    description: "`typeinfo.species`, e.g. Cruiser, Destroyer, Artillery."
                    type: string
                  tier:
                    description: "The entry's `level` field: tier for ships and modules."
                    type: integer
                    maximum: 11
                    minimum: 1
                additionalProperties: false
                required:
                  - type
            required:
              - set
            additionalProperties: false
            description: "Entries of one `typeinfo.type`, in GameParams order."
        description: Which GameParams entries to read.
      filter:
        type: string
        description: "jq program (jaq dialect) run once per selected entry, with the entry\nobject as input. Start with `keys` to list fields, then narrow, e.g.\n`.A_Hull | keys` or `.A1_Artillery | {maxDist, sigmaCount}`."
      limit:
        type: integer
        maximum: 200
        minimum: 1
        description: "Most entries a set selection reads, 1 to 200, default 20."
      max_bytes:
        type: integer
        maximum: 24000
        minimum: 500
        description: "Output budget in bytes, 500 to 24000, default 6000."
    required:
      - select
      - filter
